This is **Erdős Problem 371**, which is a well-known **open conjecture** — it is listed as open on erdosproblems.com and formalized in the "Formal Conjectures" project with `sorry` and `category research open`. The conjecture asserts that the set $\{n : P(n+1) > P(n)\}$ has natural density $1/2$.

A few observations that show why it is *plausible* but not proven:

- Since $\gcd(n, n+1) = 1$, the integers $n$ and $n+1$ share no prime factor, so $P(n) \neq P(n+1)$ for all $n \ge 2$ (no ties). Hence the sets $A = \{n : P(n+1) > P(n)\}$ and $B = \{n : P(n) > P(n+1)\}$ partition $\mathbb{N}$ (up to finitely many exceptions). If one could show $A$ and $B$ have equal density, both would be $1/2$.
- Heuristically, $P(n)$ and $P(n+1)$ behave like i.i.d. draws from the same distribution (governed by the Dickman–de Bruijn function), so by symmetry each ordering should occur half the time.
- However, **proving the joint distribution / symmetry rigorously** is the crux of the open problem. No measure-preserving involution swapping $A$ and $B$ is known, and the independence heuristic for $P(n)$ and $P(n+1)$ has not been established at the strength needed.

As this is an open research problem in analytic number theory, I cannot produce a complete proof.

